In LaTeX tables, creating a new line within a cell can be done using the \\ command. This command is placed at the end of the line within the cell, which will start a new line in the cell. Here is an example of how to use it:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Column 1} & \textbf{Column 2} \\
\hline
Cell 1, Line 1 & Cell 2, Line 1 \\ 
Cell 1, Line 2 \\ 
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a LaTeX table with a new line in a cell.}
\label{tab:example}
\end{table}

In this example, the second line of "Cell 1, Line 2" is created by placing the \\ command at the end of the first line, which tells LaTeX to start a new line in that cell. This can be useful when you need to include multi-line text in a single cell of a LaTeX table.

